# Code owners for the surfaces that grant authority.
#
# Every privilege in this system is a hand-kept list: RELEASE_FINALIZER_JOBS decides which
# job may write a ref or create a Release, SHA_PINNED_ACTIONS and
# CREDENTIAL_ACTIONS_ON_MOVING_REFS decide how much of the supply chain is pinned, and the
# workflows decide which credentials exist at all. Each is defended by tests that the same
# commit can edit, so until this file existed, one approval could move a grant and relax
# the guard that reports on it in the same diff.
#
# This file only requests review. It has teeth only with branch protection set to require
# code-owner review on the default branch -- if that is not enabled, this is documentation.
# `test_the_authority_surfaces_are_owned` asserts the coverage; nothing here can assert the
# branch-protection setting, and that limit is stated rather than implied.

# The publication pipeline itself, and the composite actions that run inside its jobs.
/.github/                                   @ravensorb

# The registries, and every guard that governs them.
/tests/ci/                                  @ravensorb

# The decisions the registries point at. An entry without an ADR is not a grant.
/docs/adr/                                  @ravensorb

# The gate configuration: which checks run, and over which files.
/.pre-commit-config.yaml                    @ravensorb
/.gitleaks.toml                             @ravensorb

# Agent instructions. Tooling is told to "READ its entire contents and follow its
# directions exactly", these trees are excluded from the linting gates, and the agents
# that read them hold write access. That combination makes them executable surface in
# every sense that matters, so they are reviewed like it.
#
# How much of that is checked, and how much is review alone. A `payload-manifest.json`
# is not an inventory of its skill: it records the shared payload the installer copied
# into that skill (`"generated_from": "skills/_shared/"`), with a declared release and a
# SHA-256 for each file it names.
# `test_the_vendored_trees_match_the_manifests_their_installer_shipped` verifies exactly
# those, offline, against the manifest already in the tree. Counted in files rather than
# in skills, that is 142 of the 2,364 files across the two trees.
#
# The other 2,222 -- including every `SKILL.md`, the file this paragraph calls
# executable surface -- are named by no manifest and verified against no upstream
# reference. This repository holds no record of "the installed release" for them, and
# one invented here would assert its own correctness. They are pinned only by `.agents/`
# and `.claude/` being byte-identical copies of one payload, which
# `test_the_vendored_trees_are_byte_identical_copies_of_each_other` asserts. An edit
# made to both copies at once passes every check and is caught here, by review, or not
# at all. `test_the_vendored_integrity_guard_records_what_it_cannot_verify` recomputes
# those counts from the trees, so this paragraph fails the gate rather than drifting.
/AGENTS.md                                  @ravensorb
/CLAUDE.md                                  @ravensorb
/.agents/                                   @ravensorb
/.claude/                                   @ravensorb
/.github/agents/                            @ravensorb
